    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-134155",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance",
      "problem": "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,8\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-5)(2-6)+(6-5)(4-6)+(8-5)(10-6)+(2-5)(8-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-4)+(1)(-2)+(3)(4)+(-3)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4-2+12-6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
