      "math_academy_id": "q-134207",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
      "problem": "| **Temperature,\nin  ${}^{∘}F\\,(x)$** | $60$ | $42$ | $80$ | $70$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Humidity, in $%\\,(y)$** | $42$ | $24$ | $30$ | $60$ |\n\nThe table above shows data Tom collected about temperature and humidity in his city. \nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=63$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=39.$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(60\\,-\\,63)(42\\,-\\,39)+(42\\,-\\,63)(24\\,-\\,39)+(80\\,-\\,63)(30\\,-\\,39)+(70\\,-\\,63)(60\\,-\\,39)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(3)+(-21)(-15)+(17)(-9)+(7)(21)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-9+315-153+147] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 300 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
