    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-149572",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
      "problem": "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $14$ | $16$ | $16$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight, in kg ($y$)** | $62$ | $63$ | $73$ | $78$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding weights of four members of a youth rock band. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=16$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=69.$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(14-16)(62-69)+(16-16)(63-69)+(16-16)(73-69)+(18-16)(78-69)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-2)(-7)+(0)(-6)+(0)(4)+(2)(9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[14+0+0+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
